// visio/sensor/v1/diag_log.proto
//
// One batch of the device's own diagnostic log, published on "/<root>/diag_log"
// beside "/<root>/system_health" — so the log lands INSIDE every recording, on
// the same clock as the footage. A customer who sends us an MCAP has sent us
// the device's account of what it was doing, whether or not they ever open a
// return.
//
// The bytes are one complete `VDLW` container (docs/protocol/diag_log.md): a
// 32-byte plaintext header and ChaCha20 ciphertext of a run of log lines. Each
// message is self-contained on purpose. A reader joins mid-stream, a stream
// policy may drop messages, an MCAP can be torn at a power cut — any of those
// makes a continuous keystream unrecoverable from that point on, so every
// message carries its own nonce and decrypts alone. The header's magic is what
// tells a reader this is a log batch and not a recording part.
//
// A sibling of SystemHealth rather than a field inside it, deliberately.
// SystemHealth is a typed message Foxglove renders; an opaque ciphertext field
// would turn a readable health panel into noise. And MCAP indexes per channel,
// so a host tool lifts the log out of a recording without touching video —
// while SetStreamPolicy can drop it by topic for a live viewer.
//
// Cadence is bursty and low: batched at ~0.2–1 Hz, hard-capped by the device so
// a kernel error storm cannot inflate every recording on a sick unit.

